# Examples

Private apps that exercise the packages the way a consumer would. Run them with `yarn workspace <name> …`; the workspace name is not the folder name.

| Folder | Workspace | Covers |
|---|---|---|
| `examples/concurrent` | `example-react-18` | SSR with streaming partial hydration via `@anansi/core` (`anansi serve`), `@anansi/router`, `@data-client/*`, antd, linaria |
| `examples/linaria` | `example-linaria` | `@anansi/webpack-config` with linaria |
| `examples/typescript` | `example-typescript` | SPA build, Jest via `@anansi/jest-preset`, `@anansi/storybook`, the `BROWSERSLIST_ENV` matrix, bundle-size comparison (`build:sizecompare`) |

- `lerna version` never versions, tags or creates GitHub releases for examples: `lerna.json` sets `command.version.private: false` (lerna's `--no-private`).
- StackBlitz demos (linked from READMEs and dataclient.io) install an example folder on its own with npm, so examples depend on `@anansi/*` with `^<current version>`, never `workspace:`. `yarn.config.cjs` enforces that (CI runs `yarn constraints`), and the root `version` script runs `yarn constraints --fix` so `yarn changelog` updates the ranges when it bumps packages.
- `example-react-18` builds the client and server bundles in `build`; `start:server` serves `dist-server/App.js`. GitHub's lighthouse workflow scores it.
- `yarn devtest:ci` starts only `example-react-18` and `example-linaria`.
- `test:type` (`tsc --noEmit`) is the only type check examples get, and CI runs it through `yarn test:type`.
- Dev server: `yarn workspace example-react-18 run start` (or `yarn start` in the folder).
- After rebuilding a package, run the example with `WEBPACK_CACHE=none` so webpack's cache doesn't serve the previous build.
